<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Kępno</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1282</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Kępno was an agreement made on 15 February 1282 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestwin-ii">Mestwin II</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii">Przemysł II</a>, Duke of Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Mestwin, who had no sons, named Przemysł his heir, and on Mestwin's death in 1294 Pomerelia passed to Przemysł, who was crowned King of Poland the next year. The treaty later underpinned Polish claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kepno.jpg" alt="Treaty of Kępno" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Matejko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Przemysl_II_(76843358)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
